from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path.cwd();H=ROOT/'doc/book/iterations/2026-09-28-foundry-a-mark-in-the-room';M=ROOT/'commons/maps/Pattern_Foundry'
read=lambda p:p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
def change(name, pairs, append=''):
 p=M/name;s=read(p)
 for old,new in pairs:
  assert old in s,(name,old)
  s=s.replace(old,new,1)
 p.write_bytes((s.rstrip()+append+'\n').encode('utf-8'))
assert (M/'final.md').read_bytes()==(H/'before/commons/maps/Pattern_Foundry/final.md').read_bytes(),'Chapter changed since baseline'
(M/'final.md').write_bytes((H/'reading-draft.md').read_bytes())
change('intent.md',[
 ('Opening hall of Patterns (tiling), following Colour.','Second hall of Patterns (tiling), following Pattern_Disco.'),
 ('Primary encounters: pattern_tile_4x4, pattern_tile_mirror, pattern_machine_a.','Primary encounters: pattern_architecture, pattern_tile_4x4, pattern_tile_mirror, pattern_machine_a.'),
 ('Action: Place two asymmetric tile marks; compare reflection; use MARK at the loom specimens; hold the curve and edit again; relink, save, change and restore the resolved source.','Action: Notice a machine output on the architecture; place two asymmetric marks in the tile preview; compare reflection; use MARK at the loom specimens; hold the curve and edit again. Then enter the curved room, paint one studio cell, hold ROOM FLOOR and edit again: wall and floor retain different moments. Source saves, offsets and wider controls belong to a return visit.'),
 ('The loom recipe, two specimen receivers and one 4.8 by 0.9 metre skin on the existing inner wall are connected.','The loom recipe, two specimen receivers and three inner-wall skins are connected. The curved room adds two studio-owned receivers, ROOM WALL and ROOM FLOOR, both live initially. Its local printed floor sits above the separate hall-wide print. DRESS THE ARCHITECTURE reads machine output textures; the tile editors have their own previews.'),
 ('Architecture currently includes three inner-wall faces; whole-hall coverage remains open.','ARCHITECTURE on the specimen console includes three inner-wall faces. The separate DRESS THE ARCHITECTURE controller covers the hall floor and exposed grid walls; its choice of machine does not reassign the studio room.')])
change('tutorial.md',[
 ('Begin with the two primary tile stations.','Pattern_Disco supplied a pattern and a palette. Here the visitor authors a source. Notice the machine output already on the hall, then begin with the two primary tile stations; their previews are separate from that architectural print.'),
 ('Whole-hall and garment adapters follow.','The separate DRESS THE ARCHITECTURE controller already supplies whole-hall floor and wall skins from machine output textures. Garment transfer remains separate.')],
 '''\n\nFor the first visit, continue from the held curve into the curved room in the annex. The room begins with both wall and floor linked to the studio, and ROOM selected on its surface console. Choose a palette colour and paint one studio cell, then follow it across the room. Select ROOM FLOOR, HOLD, and edit the source again. The floor retains its visible print while the curved wall follows. APPLY LIVE catches the floor up; UNDO LINK can recover its earlier held image. Entering, moving inside and leaving use the existing opening and museum floor. WALL alone names the older low wall beside the loom, so reserve source takeover of that receiver for a return visit.\n\nThe native check must compare the visible floor, not only its stored texture. A raised hall-wide print previously hid the local floor despite successful source updates. The architectural skins now account for the console's height; the local room print lies above them without changing physical support.''')
change('technical.md',[
 ('This is the opening Patterns hall, after Colour. Its three book encounters are `pattern_tile_4x4`, `pattern_tile_mirror` and `pattern_machine_a`.','This is the second Patterns hall, following Pattern_Disco and leading to Tutorial_Pattern. Its four book encounters are `pattern_architecture`, `pattern_tile_4x4`, `pattern_tile_mirror` and `pattern_machine_a`.'),
 ('The receiver dictionary explicitly registers five owned meshes, metre dimensions and link states.','The loom study initially registers five owned meshes, metre dimensions and link states. The opt-in curved room adds ROOM WALL and ROOM FLOOR; ALL FIVE keeps its original membership.'),
 ('Whole-hall control, gesture ownership, garment-region transfer and shader teaching remain planned upgrades.','The separate whole-hall controller is implemented (below). Gesture ownership, garment-region transfer and the later shader teaching encounter remain further work.'),
 ('The studio remains the editable source; its controls, held images and undo work in the room.','The studio remains the editable source. Both room receivers start live, with ROOM selected on the studio surface console; no APPLY LIVE step is required to establish the initial connection. Its controls, held images and undo work in the room.')],
 '''\n\n### Whole-hall print and the local floor\n\n`pattern_architecture.gd` builds two material skins from the authored grid: floor cells and exposed vertical wall faces. SOURCE selects the loom, kaleidoscope, stamp press or pattern mill output texture. The loom output includes its short carpet history. TARGET limits subsequent refreshes to ROOM, WALLS or FLOOR; excluded surfaces keep their last image. LIVE / HOLD snapshots both skins. Repeat size is 2, 4 or 8 metres per full output texture. TINT multiplies output colour; it does not edit the machine palette. RESET returns to loom/room/live/4 m/white. These meshes add no collision. The studio's two room receivers and the smaller tile previews remain separate connections.\n\nWhen the museum raises the control stand, skin vertices subtract its complete grid-local position, including height, so the print remains on the architecture. Previously only x/z were subtracted: the floor print floated at y=0.286 m in the measured run, obscuring the curved room's own y=0.022 m floor. The whole-hall print is now y=0.026 m above the museum floor, while the curved room print is y=0.04 m. This is visual layering only; neither print supplies physical support. A native rendered comparison confirms that editing the studio changes visible ground pixels, and holding ROOM FLOOR keeps those pixels while the source and wall change.''')
change('critical.md',[
 ('The colour chamber ended with one message and several receiving operations. Here one source supplies many marks.','Pattern_Disco let a shared palette change an existing field. Here the visitor can author the small source that supplies many marks.'),
 ('For Ada\'s question, what bodies are possible, the implemented answer now includes the room\'s inner wall.','For Ada\'s question, what bodies are possible, the implemented answer includes both the existing inner walls and a curved room the visitor can enter.'),
 ('As we extend the panel, that selection must remain legible: a promise that the whole hall can change should be tested against the actual surfaces included.','That selection must remain legible beside DRESS THE ARCHITECTURE, whose separate ROOM selection addresses the hall skins. ROOM on the studio addresses its curved room. These scopes have different memberships even when their labels resemble one another.')],
 '''\n\nInside the curved room, this relation surrounds the visitor. With the floor held, a new source decision travels around the wall while the ground keeps the earlier image. Both remain part of one usable room. The question can begin in the pleasure of that difference: what would you want to keep, and what would you let follow? It need not demand a complete refusal of repetition. The available separation is still coarse: a whole receiving surface can decline an update; one repeated mark cannot yet choose independently.\n\nThis encounter also exposed a material limit of a source-only audit. The floor's texture accepted edits while a higher architectural print hid them. The code relationship existed, but the visitor could not encounter it. Restoring the visible floor was necessary before asking the manuscript to build an argument on that comparison.''')
print('Promoted final.md and updated four companions.')
